## Import

`{{ .Name }}` can be imported using the connection's ID in SELECT:

```bash
terraform import {{ .Name }}.example 2f1c8b4e-9a6d-4d1f-9d0e-7d3a5b6c8e01
```

The ID is assigned by SELECT when the connection is added, so there is nothing about your GCP project that predicts it. It is the `id` attribute, and it is also the last path segment of the connection's page in the SELECT UI.

A connection reached without service account impersonation has no `service_account` on the API side, which this resource treats as required — set one in your configuration before importing a connection like that, or the first plan will show it as a change from null to a value.

## Additional Information

### DoiT-managed connections must be created in the SELECT UI

Setting `is_doit = true` tells SELECT to read the project's spend from DoiT's own billing data instead of an export in your project, and it requires `bigquery_dataset_id` and `billing_account_id` to be omitted rather than set. Adding a connection like this needs a signed-in user's credential, which an API key is not — so `terraform apply` on a new `is_doit = true` resource fails with a `403`. Add it in the SELECT UI instead, then bring it under Terraform with `terraform import`; every other operation, including toggling `is_doit`-adjacent fields like `name` and `sync_enabled`, works normally with an API key from then on.

### Creating a connection validates it against BigQuery

SELECT runs a full set of checks — connectivity, project access, region and job discovery, and the billing and pricing exports — before it stores anything. A create that fails leaves no connection behind, and the error names the checks that did not pass along with what to change and the documentation page covering it.

This means `terraform apply` needs a service account and export that actually work, and that a GCP outage will fail the apply rather than store a broken connection.

### Which changes revalidate

SELECT revalidates whenever an apply changes `gcp_project_id`, `bigquery_dataset_id`, `billing_account_id` or `service_account`. Changing only `name`, `sync_enabled` or `query_sanitization_enabled` makes no call to BigQuery, so a rename works even when BigQuery does not.

A successful revalidation re-resolves `gcp_organization_id`, `gcp_organization_name` and `regions`.

### One connection per GCP project

SELECT allows one connection per GCP project; a second one for the same project is refused with a conflict naming it. Bring the existing connection under Terraform with `terraform import` instead of adding another.

### `is_doit` cannot be changed

Whether a connection is DoiT-managed is fixed when it is added. Changing it forces Terraform to destroy the connection and create a new one, which starts the project's usage history over — and since a `true` value can only be created in the SELECT UI, that replacement is also not something `terraform apply` alone can carry out.

### `billing_account_id` is stored upper case

SELECT normalizes the Cloud Billing account ID to upper case regardless of how it was configured, so a lower-case value in your configuration does not mean a lower-case value in state — this resource tracks your configured casing without treating the difference as drift.

### Changes made outside Terraform

Every write carries the connection's `etag` as an `If-Match` header, so an update or delete is refused if the connection changed since Terraform last read it. When that happens, run `terraform apply -refresh-only` to pick up the current state and then apply again.

### Deleting a connection is not reversible

Destroying this resource removes the connection and the usage data SELECT has collected through it. Re-adding the same project starts its history over.
